 ],
 "literal": "As daylight passes, as jasmine blooms, / even the helpless evening when the sun's heat has abated, / if we have swam across with night as the limit, / what will happen, may you live, my friend? / The flood of night is greater than the ocean!",
 "translation": "As the daylight fades and the jasmine blooms, we might manage to swim across this helpless evening when the sun's fierce heat has cooled, using the night as our boundary. But what then, my friend? May you live long! The flood of the dark night is far vaster than the ocean.",
 "modern": "பகற்பொழுது கழிய, முல்லை மலர, கதிரவனின் வெப்பம் தணிந்த இந்தத் துணையற்ற மாலைப் பொழுதை இரவு எல்லையாகக் கொண்டு நாம் கடந்துவிட்டாலும், தோழியே! நீ வாழ்க! அதற்குப் பின் வரும் கங்குலாகிய வெள்ளம் கடலை விடப் பெரியது; அதை எவ்வாறு கடப்போம்?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante during the mullai (forest) season, waiting in despair for her husband's return. She laments that even if they manage to survive and cross the painful evening, which is made harder by the blooming jasmine and the cooling sun, they cannot survive the night. She describes the night as an endless flood, far vaster and deeper than the ocean itself. The imagery of the blooming jasmine and the fading light intensifies her loneliness, as these are classic signs of the evening when lovers should be together. The key words are 'mālai' (evening) and 'kaṅkul veḷḷam' (flood of night).",
